<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"> <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Archivo:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Jost:wght@500;600;700&display=swap"> {% assign p_sushi = all_products['knead-that-sushi-chunky-squishy-plush-blind-box'] %} {% assign p_ckb = all_products['click-key-blocks'] %} {% assign p_sushi_blocks = all_products['sushi-blocks-2pk-blind-bag'] %} {% assign p_ice_blocks = all_products['ice-cream-blocks-2pk-blind-bag'] %} {% assign p_bubble = all_products['endless-bubble-wrap-dark-blue'] %} {% assign p_2go = all_products['endless-bubble-2go-blind-bag'] %} {% assign p_tagd = all_products['milky-way-tag-d-2-by-watchitude'] %} {% assign knead_collection_url = routes.collections_url | append: '/knead-that' %} {% assign ice_image = section.settings.ice_image %} {% assign burger_image = section.settings.burger_image %} <div class="wt-home"> <section id="top" class="wt-section wt-hero">
  <div class="wt-hero__grid">
    <article class="wt-hero__feature" data-wt-option="sushi">
      <div class="wt-hero__media">{% if section.settings.hero_image != blank %}<img src="{{ section.settings.hero_image | image_url: width: 1600 }}" width="{{ section.settings.hero_image.width }}" height="{{ section.settings.hero_image.height }}" alt="Knead That Sushi" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--sushi">{% elsif p_sushi.featured_image %}<img src="{{ p_sushi.featured_image | image_url: width: 1600 }}" width="{{ p_sushi.featured_image.width }}" height="{{ p_sushi.featured_image.height }}" alt="{{ p_sushi.title }}" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--sushi">{% endif %}{% if ice_image != blank %}<img src="{{ ice_image | image_url: width: 1600 }}" width="{{ ice_image.width }}" height="{{ ice_image.height }}" alt="Knead That Ice Cream" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--ice">{% endif %}{% if burger_image != blank %}<img src="{{ burger_image | image_url: width: 1600 }}" width="{{ burger_image.width }}" height="{{ burger_image.height }}" alt="Knead That Burger" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--burger">{% else %}<img src="{{ 'p03-x228.jpg' | asset_url }}" width="342" height="332" alt="Knead That Burger" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--burger">{% endif %}</div>
      <div class="wt-hero__overlay">
        <p class="wt-hero__eyebrow wt-hero__eyebrow--feature">Season 2 &middot; mystery box</p>
        <h1 class="wt-hero__title">knead that <span class="wt-hero__title-word wt-hero__title-word--sushi">sushi.</span><span class="wt-hero__title-word wt-hero__title-word--ice">ice cream.</span><span class="wt-hero__title-word wt-hero__title-word--burger">burger.</span></h1>
        <p class="wt-hero__lead">Eight common styles, one rare chase. Chunky squishy plush in a sealed box &mdash; each box is a surprise!</p>
        <div class="wt-hero__actions">
          <a href="{{ p_sushi.url | default: knead_collection_url }}" class="wt-btn">Shop mystery boxes</a>
          <div class="wt-hero__pills">
            <a href="{{ knead_collection_url }}" class="wt-hero__pill wt-hero__pill--active" data-wt-option="sushi">Sushi &mdash; available now</a>
            {%- comment -%} Ice cream has no art of its own to fall back on, so with the setting empty it stays the plain, inert span it was: there is nothing to switch to. {%- endcomment -%}
            {% if ice_image != blank %}<button type="button" class="wt-hero__pill" data-wt-option="ice">Ice cream &mdash; Sept&nbsp;15</button>{% else %}<span class="wt-hero__pill">Ice cream &mdash; Sept&nbsp;15</span>{% endif %}
            <button type="button" class="wt-hero__pill" data-wt-option="burger">Burger &mdash; Oct&nbsp;15</button>
          </div>
          <span class="wt-hero__price">{{ p_sushi.price | money_without_trailing_zeros | default: '$20' }} each</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
    <div class="wt-hero__side">
      <article class="wt-hero__card" data-wt-option="full">
        <div class="wt-hero__media"><img src="{{ 'p15-x1200.png' | asset_url }}" width="1200" height="1200" alt="Endless Bubble Wrap, full size" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--full"><img src="{{ 'p14-x1116.png' | asset_url }}" width="1200" height="1200" alt="Endless Bubble 2Go blind bag" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--2go"></div>
        <div class="wt-hero__card-overlay">
          <p class="wt-hero__eyebrow">Never runs out</p>
          <h2 class="wt-hero__card-title"><a href="{{ routes.collections_url }}/endless-bubble" class="wt-hero__card-link">endless bubble</a></h2>
          <p class="wt-hero__card-copy">Two sizes &mdash; 2Go blind bag {{ p_2go.price | money_without_trailing_zeros | default: '$13.50' }}, full size {{ p_bubble.price | money_without_trailing_zeros | default: '$20' }}</p>
          <div class="wt-hero__pills wt-hero__pills--compact">
            <button type="button" class="wt-hero__pill wt-hero__pill--compact wt-hero__pill--active" data-wt-option="full">Full size</button>
            <button type="button" class="wt-hero__pill wt-hero__pill--compact" data-wt-option="2go">2Go</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
      {%- comment -%}
        The dots carry each product's name, and the photos behind them are
        alt="", so a screen reader hears the nine names once from the
        controls rather than nine times from images that are all the same
        watch. Sighted readers get the same name from the line above the
        dots, which swaps with them. Nine is the whole tag'd collection
        and the cap the product row in #tagd uses; the CSS that shows a
        slide is written out to match it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% assign tagd_hero_products = collections['tagd'].products %}
      {% assign tagd_collection_url = routes.collections_url | append: '/tagd' %}
      {%- comment -%}
        The nine tag'd photos are all 1000x1000 with the watch dead
        centre, so the frames already line up -- but the watch is not
        shot at one size inside them. Measured off the face, the discs
        run from 256px (Pink Icing) to 278px (Army Camo) on a 900px
        render: a 9% spread, which is what makes the watch jump a little
        as the slides cross-fade rather than sit still.

        A correction per photo, scaling each one up until its face
        matches the largest. Up rather than down on purpose: cover
        already fills the card exactly, so anything under 1 would pull
        the photo off the card's edges and show a band of mist round it,
        the way the sushi shot's 0.9 does deliberately. The straps run
        off the corners anyway, so the extra crop costs nothing.

        Keyed on handle, not on position: the slide CSS below has to go
        by index and breaks if the collection is reordered, but these
        numbers belong to particular photographs and would be actively
        wrong on the wrong one. A style not listed here scales by 1,
        which is the right default for a new photo shot to any size --
        it just will not be corrected until someone measures it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign tagd_scales = 'milky-way-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.049,army-camo-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1,master-builder-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.069,score-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.071,heart-balloons-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.069,wings-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.082,pink-icing-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.086,unicorn-love-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.069,over-the-rainbow-tag-d-2-by-watchitude:1.045' | split: ',' -%}
      <article class="wt-hero__card wt-hero__card--dark" data-wt-option="0">
        <div class="wt-hero__media wt-hero__media--dim">{% if tagd_hero_products.size > 0 %}{% for product in tagd_hero_products limit: 9 %}{%- assign tagd_scale = '1' -%}{%- for pair in tagd_scales -%}{%- assign kv = pair | split: ':' -%}{%- if kv[0] == product.handle -%}{%- assign tagd_scale = kv[1] -%}{%- endif -%}{%- endfor -%}<img src="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 900 }}" width="{{ product.featured_image.width }}" height="{{ product.featured_image.height }}" alt="" class="wt-hero__img wt-hero__img--switch wt-hero__img--tagd-{{ forloop.index0 }}"{% unless tagd_scale == '1' %} style="transform: scale({{ tagd_scale }})"{% endunless %}>{% endfor %}{% elsif p_tagd.featured_image %}<img src="{{ p_tagd.featured_image | image_url: width: 900 }}" width="{{ p_tagd.featured_image.width }}" height="{{ p_tagd.featured_image.height }}" alt="{{ p_tagd.title }}" class="wt-hero__img">{% endif %}</div>
        <div class="wt-hero__card-overlay">
          <p class="wt-hero__eyebrow">Works with Apple AirTag</p>
          {%- comment -%}
            The link is the style on show, not the collection it came
            from: the card is a picture of one watch, and clicking a
            picture of one watch should land on that watch. The dots
            carry the url of theirs and the switcher moves it here, so
            it follows the photo through all nine.

            Rendered with the first product's url rather than left for
            the script to fill in, so the card is already pointing
            somewhere true before any of this runs -- and falls back to
            the collection if the collection is empty, which is the same
            thing the media does one line up. "Shop Tag'd" below is what
            now carries the job this link used to do.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <h2 class="wt-hero__card-title"><a href="{{ tagd_hero_products.first.url | default: tagd_collection_url }}" class="wt-hero__card-link" data-wt-card-link>tag'd 2</a></h2>
          <p class="wt-hero__card-copy">A slap watch with a pocket &mdash; {{ p_tagd.price | money_without_trailing_zeros | default: '$30' }}</p>
          {% if tagd_hero_products.size > 1 %}<p class="wt-hero__style">{%- for product in tagd_hero_products limit: 9 -%}<span class="wt-hero__style-word wt-hero__style-word--{{ forloop.index0 }}">{{ product.title | split: ' - ' | first | escape }}</span>{%- endfor -%}</p>
          {%- comment -%}
            Dots and button on the card's last line, which is why they
            are wrapped rather than stacked: the row is what puts one at
            each end of it.

            The button is the same offer #knead makes under its nine
            character tiles, in the same words and the same button. It
            matters more here than it does there, because the card's own
            link stopped going to the collection when it started going
            to the watch.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="wt-hero__controls">
            <div class="wt-hero__pills wt-hero__pills--compact wt-hero__pills--dots">
              {%- for product in tagd_hero_products limit: 9 -%}
              <button type="button" class="wt-hero__pill wt-hero__pill--dot{% if forloop.first %} wt-hero__pill--active{% endif %}" data-wt-option="{{ forloop.index0 }}" data-wt-url="{{ product.url }}" aria-pressed="{% if forloop.first %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}" aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }}"></button>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
            <a href="{{ tagd_collection_url }}" class="wt-btn wt-btn--compact">Shop Tag'd</a>
          </div>{% endif %}
        </div>
        {%- comment -%}
          Sibling of the overlay rather than a child of it, so it can sit
          against the card's right edge instead of inside the 28px the
          copy is inset by. Which also keeps it clear of the overlay's
          pointer-events: none, and lets it paint after the stretched
          card link -- both of which the dots have to solve for
          themselves further down the stylesheet.

          Same guard as the dots: one style is nothing to cycle.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {% if tagd_hero_products.size > 1 %}<button type="button" class="wt-hero__next" data-wt-next aria-label="Next style"></button>{% endif %}
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% if p_ckb != blank %}
<section id="new" class="wt-section wt-new">
  {% render 'wt-new', handle: 'click-key-blocks', title: 'click-key blocks', cta: 'Shop Click-Key Blocks', copy: section.settings.new_copy, fallback_image: section.settings.new_image, fallback_label: 'Click-Key Blocks photo &mdash; SKU 740' %}
</section>
{% endif %}
{%- comment -%}
  Sits here rather than down by #trust, where it used to: it is the
  only band on the page that sorts the catalogue instead of selling one
  thing, so it reads as a way in to everything below it. Left at the
  bottom it was a summary of a page the visitor had already scrolled.

  It is not inside the p_ckb guard above -- the two are unrelated, and
  the play grid stands on its own if the Click-Key band is absent.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% render 'wt-play' %}
<section id="knead" class="wt-section wt-knead">
  <div class="wt-knead__grid">
    <div class="wt-knead__frame">
      <div class="wt-knead__media">{% if p_sushi.featured_image %}<img src="{{ p_sushi.featured_image | image_url: width: 1200 }}" width="{{ p_sushi.featured_image.width }}" height="{{ p_sushi.featured_image.height }}" alt="{{ p_sushi.title }}" class="wt-knead__img">{% endif %}</div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="wt-knead__eyebrow">Season 2</p>
      <h2 class="wt-knead__title">nine to find. one is rare.</h2>
      <p class="wt-knead__body">Every flavour has eight common styles and one chase. In sushi that's Poppy, the special roll. Boxes are sealed, so the only way to know is to open one.</p>
      <div class="wt-knead__styles">
        {% assign kt_characters = 'Ume, Kona, Colby, Yogi, Jet, Sakura, Suki, Jade, Poppy' | split: ', ' %}
        {% for name in kt_characters %}<div class="wt-knead__tile"><img src="{{ name | downcase | prepend: 'kt-' | append: '.png' | asset_url }}" width="300" height="300" alt="{{ name }}" class="wt-knead__tile-img"></div>{% endfor %}
